# OS/7 — a REAL Active Directory domain controller, in a container.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. Everything OS/7 says about Active Directory is otherwise a
# statement about code: an LDAP bind that is never made, a control that is never
# sent, a password that is never accepted. This repository's rule is that a
# diagnostic must be checked against the thing it claims to check, and for a
# directory client the thing is a directory server that answers.
#
# Samba can act as an AD DC. That gives this project a domain controller which
# is local, free, reproducible and offline — the same constraints as every other
# harness here (CLAUDE.md: "Everything runs locally. No cloud, no paid
# services."). It is NOT Windows Server, and the difference is written down in
# docs/AD-PLAN.md rather than glossed over: Samba implements the protocols, so
# LDAP, Kerberos, the password-modify path and the join are exercised for real,
# while anything Microsoft-specific above the protocol is not.
#
# WHAT IS DELIBERATELY REAL HERE, and the reason (same argument as
# Dockerfile.check-update's gnupg): faking an LDAP server would test only the
# fake. The interesting failures — a server that refuses a password change over
# an unencrypted channel, a referral, a paged search that stops at 1000, a
# GSSAPI bind against a KDC — cannot be produced by a stub, because they are
# exactly the behaviours nobody would think to write into one.
#
# PROVISIONING HAPPENS AT RUN TIME, not here. `samba-tool domain provision`
# writes the DC's own A record, and the address a container gets is not known
# until it is started. Provisioning at build time produces a DC whose DNS
# answers with an address that nothing is listening on — a server that is up and
# unreachable, reporting no error. Roughly 30 seconds per start, paid once.
FROM ubuntu:26.04

ENV DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive

# THE PACKAGE THAT RUNS AN AD DC IS NOT `samba`, AND THIS COST A BUILD.
# Measured on resolute, 2026-08-27, after the first version of this file
# installed `samba` and the entrypoint died with `samba-tool: not found`:
#
#   samba          Suggests: python3-samba          <- and samba-tool is IN it
#   samba          Breaks:   samba-ad-dc, samba-ad-provision
#   samba-ad-dc    Depends:  samba, python3-samba, winbind, samba-dsdb-modules
#   samba-ad-provision                              <- the provision templates
#
# So `apt-get install --no-install-recommends samba` yields a file server whose
# `samba-tool` is absent, and the AD role has been split into its own packages.
# The failure is at run time, in a container that had already built green.
# Names the role, not the daemon: samba-ad-dc pulls the daemon in behind it.
#
# samba-vfs-modules is a transitional package on this release and is dropped.
#
# krb5-user             kinit/klist INSIDE THE DC container only. The client side
#                       deliberately does not get them for free — whether OS/7
#                       needs them at all is one of the questions being measured
# ldb-tools             ldbsearch: the second opinion. The checks confirm what
#                       OS/7 wrote by reading the database with a tool that
#                       shares no code with the client under test
# ldap-utils            ldapsearch, for the same reason at the protocol level
RUN apt-get update -qq \
 && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \
      samba-ad-dc samba-ad-provision samba-dsdb-modules \
      krb5-user ldb-tools ldap-utils \
      python3 ca-certificates iproute2 dnsutils procps \
 && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/* \
 && command -v samba-tool >/dev/null \
 && samba-tool domain provision --help >/dev/null

COPY ad-dc-entrypoint.sh /usr/local/bin/os7-ad-dc
RUN chmod 0755 /usr/local/bin/os7-ad-dc

# 53   the AD SRV records every client discovery path starts from
# 88   Kerberos, 464 kpasswd
# 389  LDAP, 636 LDAPS — the password-modify path needs the second one
EXPOSE 53/tcp 53/udp 88/tcp 88/udp 389/tcp 464/tcp 636/tcp

ENTRYPOINT ["/usr/local/bin/os7-ad-dc"]
